      "predictive_factors": {
        "Temporal Dynamics for prediction": "The series shows a sharp upward regime shift: from 4.9 to 5.4 over four months, then a spike to 6.3, followed by a plateau at 6.1. Momentum is positive but decelerating; volatility increased markedly after the spike. Lagged values suggest persistence at the 6.1 level, with potential for further increases if the shock is not absorbed.",
        "External Drivers for prediction": "The September 11 attacks triggered immediate layoffs in aviation, hospitality, finance, and retail. Policy responses (e.g., emergency spending, Fed rate cuts) may cushion but not reverse the shock in the short term. Labor market tightness prior to the attacks was already weakening.",
        "Domain Signals for prediction": "Race-specific disparities are likely to widen: Black and Hispanic workers are overrepresented in vulnerable sectors (leisure, hospitality, transportation) and typically experience larger unemployment increases during downturns. The pre-existing gap may amplify the aggregate rise.",
        "Textual Signals for prediction": "News sentiment is strongly negative, emphasizing prolonged disruption and broad layoffs. Topics center on attack aftermath, sectoral shutdowns, and uncertainty. This suggests continued upward pressure on unemployment rates across racial groups, with minority groups hit hardest."
      },
      "forecast_outlook": {
        "expected_direction": "Likely up for all race groups, with Black and Hispanic unemployment rising more sharply than White.",
        "key_driver": "Post-9/11 labor-market disruption and layoffs in service and transportation sectors.",
        "confidence": "Medium, because the spike is evident but policy responses and timing of recovery are uncertain.",
        "uncertainty": "The duration of the shock and the effectiveness of fiscal/monetary stimulus in preventing further increases.",
        "horizon": "Next month, focusing on whether the plateau at 6.1 holds or breaks upward."
      }
    },
    "correction": "The original reasoning erred by treating a temporary shock as a permanent regime shift and extrapolating a monotonic upward trend, ignoring mean reversion, policy lags, and the cyclical nature of unemployment. It also failed to account for the fact that the spike was already decelerating and that racial disparities do not widen indefinitely.",
